174:- §3.2, *Assumptions of a term*: a claim inherits the assumptions of the data, design, variables and setting its evidence's setup names (roles `cases`, `procedure`, `lens`, `context`), and, of the things studied (role `studied`), only those of its own subject: the term its `about.subject` names, or each member when the subject is a group. The comparator's assumptions are not the claim's. A claim without `about` inherits from every term its setup names, as before.
195:  - §3.2: a definition lists the `assumptions` the thing it names rests on (the error distribution of a model, the randomisation of a design); evidence whose setup names it inherits them. New rule 73; warning 15 counts them.
209:A reader looking for papers asks what they studied, on what, how, and what they found about it: "which papers compare ETS with ARIMA on the M4 data by MASE", "what is known about role ambiguity among rural nurses". Until v0.29 the answer was spread over three partial records of the same thing (free-text `scope`, the forecasting-shaped `setup` of evidence, the closed lists of the qualitative profile), and the atom that §8 asks a writer to form first was never written down. This version records one frame of a study, in terms that fit any empirical design, and records each claim's atom over that frame, so that papers and claims can be found by the things they are about, whatever their field or method.
211:- §3.4, *Setup*: the roles are renamed and generalised so that they fit quantitative, qualitative, simulation and mixed designs: `studied` (was `compared`), `cases` (was `data`), `procedure` (was `design`), `lens` (was `measures`), and a new role `context`. A table maps the roles onto PICO (trials) and SPIDER (qualitative evidence synthesis). Setup is recommended for every evidence object except formal ones. Rule 61 revised; new warning 28.
262:An evaluation is made of parts that many claims share and that a reader, a reviewer or another paper needs to name one at a time: what is compared, on what data, by which design, measured how. And a sentence of a paper often carries more than one claim.
264:- Evidence gains `setup`: the parts of the evaluation, each a definition, by role: `compared`, `data`, `design`, `measures` (§3.4). New rule 61.
419:A format for research papers whose primary reader is an LLM. Humans access content by asking an LLM, so human readability is not a design goal.
500:- **Kept:** the title, licence, source of a converted paper, declarations, contributions, highlights and exploration counts; definitions with their kind, members, the wholes they are part of, their broader terms, assumptions and identifiers; claims with their type, role, novelty (when not `no_prior_found`), support with its pointers and locators, assumptions, `depends_on`, limitations, warrant, strength, verification, verifiability, planning, outcome, scope, relations with their notes (relations within the paper as well), assumptions inherited from the terms they rest on (§3.2, *Assumptions of a term*), and test; evidence with its description, design fields, setup, what it is derived from, estimate, observations (results and tables, with their values, units, conditions and where a value was read from) and proof steps; warrants; limitations; open questions; artefact descriptions, figure functions and the observations a figure shows; references.
660:- `funding`: list of `{funder, funder_id, award, kind, role}`: the funder's name, its identifier (a ROR ID such as `ror:00k4n6c32`, or a Crossref Funder Registry DOI), the award or grant number, the `kind` of support, `grant` (money) or `in_kind` (materials, vouchers, services, equipment, analyses paid for), and the funder's `role` in the work as text ("no role in design, analysis or the decision to publish"). An empty list declares no funding.
710:| `assumptions` | list of claim IDs | Assumption claims (§3.8) that the thing the term names rests on wherever the paper uses it: a model's error distribution, a design's randomisation, a data set's representativeness (below) |
727:| `design` | how a study or evaluation is arranged: trial or survey design, evaluation scheme, sampling and data collection, analytic approach | rolling origin with 8 origins; a cluster-randomised trial; purposive sampling with semi-structured interviews; reflexive thematic analysis |
739:A term the paper introduces as a finding (a theme, a typology, a newly named phenomenon) is a `concept`. The paper's own method or model is a `method`, defined in full as below. An instrument (a questionnaire, a scale, an interview guide, a coding frame) is defined as the `variable` it records, or as the `design` it is part of, and the document itself is an artefact (§3.6). (The kind `variable` was `measure` in v0.30 and v0.31.)
